<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
	TLS trust for the coop control plane.

	The relay hands out the room's media key and each participant's session secret,
	so its control plane has to be HTTPS on any network you do not own. Android
	blocks cleartext for targetSdk >= 28 outright, so there is nothing to relax here:
	what this file does is make a *LAN* certificate chain trusted, so the relay can
	serve HTTPS on a private address without a public certificate.

	debug-overrides, deliberately: it applies only to a debuggable APK, so a release
	build falls back to the system trust anchors and a real certificate. Generate the
	chain with coop-relay/scripts/lan-cert.sh, which writes the CA to
	res/raw/coop_relay_ca.pem (referenced below as @raw/coop_relay_ca) and prints the
	relay's TLS_*_FILE settings.

	One caveat worth knowing before debugging a trust failure: this fork's
	HTTPManager installs TLSSocketFactory as the default, and its LETrustManager
	delegates to the platform trust manager obtained with a null KeyStore. On Android
	that factory does honour this configuration, but the delegation is not something
	verified on a Quest yet - if the coop self-test reports a TLS failure, that
	indirection is the first suspect, and a certificate from a public authority under
	a real hostname needs none of this.
-->
<network-security-config>
	<debug-overrides>
		<trust-anchors>
			<certificates src="@raw/coop_relay_ca" />
			<certificates src="system" />
		</trust-anchors>
	</debug-overrides>
</network-security-config>
